guide.title: How UniSearch works in simple words
guide.roi.title: ROI (return on investment)
guide.roi.p1: ROI compares the estimated early-career graduate salary with the estimated cost of one study year.
guide.roi.p2: In simple terms, it helps you see whether the financial outcome looks strong relative to what you pay for education.
guide.roi.p3: ROI is a planning signal, not a salary promise. Real outcomes depend on your major, country, skills, work rights, and the job market.
guide.roi.p4: UniSearch shows ROI only when verified graduate salary data is available. If a university does not publish reliable salary data, the ROI block is hidden.
guide.roi.p5: Use ROI together with tuition, aid, admission chance, and program fit. A cheaper university is not always better, and a high salary estimate does not remove admission or visa risk.
guide.comparison.title: University comparison
guide.comparison.p1: Comparison is inside Universities, in the Comparing tab. The catalog keeps normal navigation, so selecting universities for comparison happens only in that dedicated tab.
guide.comparison.p2: Open Comparing, choose universities from cards or the map, then press Compare. Clicking a selected card again removes it from the shortlist.
guide.comparison.p3: The results page scans comparable published parameters: ranking, cost, aid, acceptance, admission categories, requirement profiles, funding, common exams, language minimums, bachelor programs, study formats, salary, and data confidence.
guide.comparison.p4: Key differences explain practical reasons to choose each university. Overview scores are relative only to the selected universities, and the detailed table highlights the strongest value in each row.
guide.comparison.note: Missing official data is shown as unavailable instead of being invented. Use comparison as a decision aid together with official university sources.
